newtonian_mechanics.latex:
\section{Newtonian Mechanics}

Newtonian mechanics is the foundation of \textbf{classical physics}, describing the motion of macroscopic objects at everyday speeds. It was formulated by Isaac Newton in his landmark work \textit{Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica} (1687).

\section{The Three Laws}

Everything in classical mechanics flows from three deceptively simple laws:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{First Law (Inertia):} An object remains at rest or in uniform motion unless acted upon by a net external force.
\item \textbf{Second Law (Force):} The net force on an object equals the rate of change of its momentum: $F = ma$
\item \textbf{Third Law (Action-Reaction):} For every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction.
\end{itemize}

\section{Core Concepts}

Explore the foundational pillars of classical mechanics:

\begin{itemize}
\item [[kinematics]] --- The geometry of motion, without worrying about forces
\item [[dynamics]] --- How forces cause and shape motion
\item [[energy_work]] --- Energy, work, and the conservation laws
\item [[momentum]] --- Linear and angular momentum
\end{itemize}

