\section{Why It Matters}

Newtonian mechanics remains the workhorse of engineering, astronomy, and everyday physics. Its limits --- near light speed or at quantum scales --- led directly to relativity and quantum mechanics.

kinematics.latex:
\section{Kinematics}

Kinematics is the branch of mechanics that describes \textbf{motion purely geometrically} --- positions, velocities, and accelerations --- without asking \emph{why} the object moves.

\section{Key Quantities}

\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|}
\hline
Quantity & Symbol & Unit \\
\hline
Position & $\vec{r}$ & m \\
Velocity & $\vec{v} = \dot{\vec{r}}$ & m/s \\
Acceleration & $\vec{a} = \ddot{\vec{r}}$ & m/s$^2$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

\section{Equations of Motion (Constant Acceleration)}

For uniform acceleration $a$:

\[ v = v_0 + at \]

\[ x = x_0 + v_0 t + \tfrac{1}{2} a t^2 \]

\[ v^2 = v_0^2 + 2a(x - x_0) \]

\section{Projectile Motion}

A classic application: under gravity $g$, horizontal and vertical motion are \textbf{independent}. The trajectory is a parabola.

\[ x(t) = v_0 \cos\theta \cdot t, \quad y(t) = v_0 \sin\theta \cdot t - \tfrac{1}{2} g t^2 \]

\section{Related Topics}

\begin{itemize}
\item [[newtonian_mechanics]] --- Back to the overview
\item [[dynamics]] --- Add forces to the picture
\item [[reference_frames]] --- How motion looks from different perspectives
\end{itemize}

dynamics.latex:
\section{Dynamics}

Dynamics studies the \textbf{relationship between forces and motion}. While kinematics asks \emph{how} an object moves, dynamics asks \emph{why}.

\section{Newton's Second Law in Depth}

The central equation of dynamics:

\[ \vec{F}_{\mathrm{net}} = m\vec{a} = \frac{d\vec{p}}{dt} \]

This is actually a \textbf{differential equation} --- solving it given initial conditions and force laws yields the full trajectory.

\section{Runtime Sanity Snippet}

\begin{pycode}
import math
m = 2.0
a = 9.81
F = m * a
print(f"F = m*a = {F:.2f} N")
print("sin(pi/2) =", math.sin(math.pi / 2))
\end{pycode}

\section{Common Force Laws}

\subsection{Gravity (Near Earth)}
\[ \vec{F}_g = -mg\,\hat{\jmath} \]

\subsection{Hooke's Law (Spring)}
\[ \vec{F}_s = -k\vec{x} \]

\subsection{Friction}
\[ f_k = \mu_k N, \quad f_s \leq \mu_s N \]

\section{Free Body Diagrams}

The essential tool: isolate the object, draw \textbf{every} force acting on it, then write $\sum F = ma$ for each direction.

\section{Lagrangian Mechanics}

A more powerful reformulation of dynamics using energy rather than forces:

\[ \mathcal{L} = T - V \]

The equations of motion follow from the [[lagrangian]] principle of least action.

\section{Related Topics}

\begin{itemize}
\item [[newtonian_mechanics]] --- The three laws
\item [[kinematics]] --- Describing the resulting motion
\item [[energy_work]] --- Energy perspective on dynamics
\end{itemize}

energy_work.latex:
\section{Energy \& Work}

Energy is the most powerful unifying concept in all of physics. The \textbf{work-energy theorem} bridges forces and energy.

\section{Work}

Work done by a force over a displacement:

\[ W = \int_{\vec{r}_1}^{\vec{r}_2} \vec{F} \cdot d\vec{r} \]

For a constant force: $W = Fd\cos\theta$

\section{Kinetic Energy}

The energy of motion:

\[ K = \tfrac{1}{2} m v^2 \]

\textbf{Work-Energy Theorem:} $W_{\mathrm{net}} = \Delta K$

\section{Potential Energy}

For \textbf{conservative forces}, we can define potential energy $U$ such that:

\[ \vec{F} = -\nabla U \]

\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
Type & Potential Energy \\
\hline
Gravity & $U = mgh$ \\
Spring & $U = \tfrac{1}{2} k x^2$ \\
Gravitational (general) & $U = -\dfrac{GMm}{r}$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

\section{Conservation of Energy}

For a closed system with only conservative forces:

\[ E = K + U = \text{constant} \]

This is one of the most profound statements in physics.

\section{Power}

\[ P = \frac{dW}{dt} = \vec{F} \cdot \vec{v} \]

\section{Related Topics}

\begin{itemize}
\item [[dynamics]] --- Force perspective
\item [[momentum]] --- The other great conserved quantity
\item [[lagrangian]] --- Energy-based formulation of all mechanics
\end{itemize}

momentum.latex:
\section{Momentum}

Momentum is a \textbf{conserved vector quantity} that captures the ``quantity of motion'' of a system.

\section{Linear Momentum}

\[ \vec{p} = m\vec{v} \]

Newton's second law in its most general form:

\[ \vec{F}_{\mathrm{net}} = \frac{d\vec{p}}{dt} \]

\section{Conservation of Linear Momentum}

When the net external force on a system is zero:

\[ \vec{p}_{\mathrm{total}} = \text{constant} \]

This governs \textbf{collisions} and \textbf{explosions}.

\subsection{Elastic vs Inelastic Collisions}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Elastic:} Both momentum and kinetic energy conserved
\item \textbf{Inelastic:} Only momentum conserved; kinetic energy lost to heat/deformation
\item \textbf{Perfectly inelastic:} Objects stick together
\end{itemize}

\section{Impulse}

The impulse-momentum theorem:

\[ \vec{J} = \int \vec{F}\, dt = \Delta\vec{p} \]

\section{Angular Momentum}

\[ \vec{L} = \vec{r} \times \vec{p} = I\vec{\omega} \]

Conserved when net \textbf{torque} is zero --- this is why a spinning ice skater speeds up when pulling their arms in.

\section{Related Topics}

\begin{itemize}
\item [[newtonian_mechanics]] --- Home
\item [[energy_work]] --- The other great conservation law
\item [[dynamics]] --- Forces and their effects
\end{itemize}

lagrangian.latex:
\section{Lagrangian Mechanics}

Lagrangian mechanics is an elegant \textbf{reformulation of classical mechanics} that replaces forces with energy --- making it vastly more powerful for complex systems.

\section{The Lagrangian}

Defined as kinetic minus potential energy:

\[ \mathcal{L}(q, \dot{q}, t) = T - V \]

where $q$ are \textbf{generalized coordinates} --- any coordinates that describe the system's configuration.

\section{The Euler--Lagrange Equations}

The equations of motion follow from the \textbf{principle of stationary action}:

\[ \frac{d}{dt}\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial \dot{q}_i} - \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial q_i} = 0 \]

One equation per degree of freedom. For a single particle in 1D, this recovers $F = ma$.

\section{Why It's Powerful}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Coordinate-free:} Choose any convenient coordinates --- polar, spherical, anything
\item \textbf{Constraints are automatic:} No need to explicitly handle normal forces
\item \textbf{Symmetry $\to$ Conservation:} Noether's theorem connects every symmetry to a conserved quantity
\end{enumerate}

\section{Example: Simple Pendulum}

Using angle $\theta$ as the generalized coordinate:

\[ \mathcal{L} = \tfrac{1}{2} m \ell^2 \dot{\theta}^2 - mg\ell(1-\cos\theta) \]

The Euler--Lagrange equation gives:

\[ \ddot{\theta} + \frac{g}{\ell}\sin\theta = 0 \]

\section{Related Topics}

\begin{itemize}
\item [[dynamics]] --- Newtonian approach to forces
\item [[energy_work]] --- The energy quantities used here
\item [[newtonian_mechanics]] --- Where it all begins
\end{itemize}

reference_frames.latex:
\section{Reference Frames}

A \textbf{reference frame} is a coordinate system relative to which we measure positions, velocities, and accelerations.

\section{Inertial vs Non-Inertial Frames}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Inertial frame:} Newton's laws hold as-is. Any frame moving at constant velocity relative to an inertial frame is also inertial.
\item \textbf{Non-inertial frame:} Accelerating frames where \textbf{fictitious forces} appear (centrifugal, Coriolis, Euler forces).
\end{itemize}

\section{Galilean Transformations}

Between two inertial frames $S$ and $S'$ where $S'$ moves at velocity $V$ relative to $S$:

\[ x' = x - Vt, \quad t' = t \]

\[ v' = v - V \]

Velocities \textbf{add linearly} --- this breaks down near the speed of light (special relativity).

\section{The Rotating Frame}

In a frame rotating with angular velocity $\vec{\omega}$, Newton's second law becomes:

\[ m\vec{a}_{\mathrm{rot}} = \vec{F} - 2m\,\vec{\omega}\times\vec{v} - m\,\vec{\omega}\times(\vec{\omega}\times\vec{r}) \]

The extra terms are the \textbf{Coriolis force} and \textbf{centrifugal force} --- they're fictitious but have real effects (hurricanes, Foucault pendulum).

\section{Related Topics}

\begin{itemize}
\item [[kinematics]] --- Motion description
\item [[newtonian_mechanics]] --- The laws that depend on frame choice
\end{itemize}
